 ],
 "literal": "Listen, may you live long, friend! The chief / himself would not dare to do this. Did I, / due to my foolishness, having associated with that noble man, / do something painful to him? / Through the water-path where strong sharks roam, / he does not know to make his coming last only a few days.",
 "translation": "Listen, my friend, may you live long! The chief would never choose to do this on his own. Could it be that I, in my foolishness, did something to hurt that noble man when we met? For he does not know how to limit his visits to just a few days, crossing the perilous sea-path where powerful sharks roam.",
 "modern": "தோழியே, நீ வாழ்க! நம் தலைவன் தானாக இப்படித் துணியமாட்டான். என்னுடைய அறியாமையினால், அந்தப் பெருந்தகையோடு பழகி, அவருக்கு வருத்தம் தரும்படியான காரியம் ஏதேனும் செய்துவிட்டேனோ? வலிமையான சுறா மீன்கள் நடமாடும் கடற்பாதையில், சில நாட்கள் மட்டுமே வந்துபோகும் வழக்கத்தை அவன் அறியவில்லையே!",
 "explanation": "This poem is set in the coastal landscape (neytal tiṇai), where the friend (tōḻi) speaks to the heroine (talaivi) while the hero is listening nearby in secret. The friend expresses deep anxiety over the hero's dangerous nightly journeys across the shark-infested sea to meet the heroine. She wonders aloud if she committed some foolish mistake during their meetings that causes the noble hero to continuously risk his life instead of seeking a permanent marriage. By highlighting the terrifying presence of strong sharks in the water-path, the friend indirectly urges the hidden hero to stop these perilous nocturnal visits and formalize their union. The key words 'vaya' (strong), 'cuṟā' (shark), and 'attam' (difficult path) emphasize the physical dangers of the coastal landscape, reflecting the emotional distress of the women.",
